   "definition": "The 357 counties of the 11 former Confederate states (Alabama, Arkansas, Florida, Georgia, Louisiana, Mississippi, North Carolina, South Carolina, Tennessee, Texas and Virginia) where Black residents were at least 25% of the population in the 2020 census. Rule from Wimberley (2010), Journal of Rural Social Sciences 25(1), who applies it to 1980; researchers use other cutoffs (25, 33, 40%), and this page uses the lowest, so the region is wide. Kentucky, Maryland, Delaware and Oklahoma are not counted. Black means Black or African American alone; people of two or more races are not counted as Black.",
   "source": "Census Bureau, 2020 Census Redistricting Data (P.L. 94-171) Summary File, table P1 (Race): P1_004N, Black or African American alone, over P1_001N, total population, by county. Threshold: Dale W. Wimberley, \"Quality of Life Trends in the Southern Black Belt, 1980-2005: A Research Note,\" Journal of Rural Social Sciences 25(1), 2010, pp. 108-109 (cited second-hand via Wikipedia, \"Black Belt in the American South\"; the PDF could not be read). Virginia counties and independent cities are separate counties here, as in geometry.json."
